  "headline": "Oil-led inflation, not fear, is driving yields and the dollar higher",
  "body": [
    "The configuration is an energy-led inflation squeeze on an economy that is not yet slowing: Brent at $107, up 75% year-to-date and 26% over three months, the 10-year at 5.22% after a 105 basis point rise this year, and the dollar index at 95% of its 52-week range. The Middle East supply channel is the driver — Drop Site News reports Ansarallah threatening to strike Aramco and Iran tightening control of the Strait of Hormuz — and bonds are pricing it as inflation to be fought, not as a growth shock to be cushioned.",
    "The case that growth is holding sits in the relationships. Copper is at 94% of its yearly range, up 20% year-to-date, and the copper/gold ratio has risen 8.5% over the month; bitcoin has outrun gold by 13.5% in the same period and is up 34% in three months in a calm volatility regime. That is liquidity and risk appetite, not a flight to safety. Gold confirms it: down 4.7% on the month and at just 17% of its range while oil and yields rise, so the metal is trading off real rates rather than fear. The curve adds the policy read — the 30-10 spread has flattened 3 basis points over the month as the 10-year rose more than the long bond, the signature of markets expecting rates to lean against inflation rather than a fiscal term-premium blowout.",
    "The tensions are in currencies. USD/CAD at a 52-week high with Brent above $100 is wrong on first principles; the resolution is that a dollar up 3% on the month is overwhelming the commodity support, and the dollar story outweighs the oil story. The rupee's 1.6% jump today is the outlier for an oil importer and looks exposed. This week's 5 basis point dip in the 10-year against a 4.8% rise in Brent is the one hint bonds may be starting to read oil as demand-destroying; the month's trend remains the better guide.",
    "Directionally, further escalation along the lines Drop Site News describes is bullish for Brent, bearish for Treasuries at the 10-year point, supportive of the dollar and a headwind for the rupee. Brent's realized volatility at 1.2 times its own-year median says the market is pricing the risk but has not treated it as resolved."
  ],
